   "text": "Medications and Medical Devices in a Power Outage | GOES \n The short answer\n The time to protect your medicines and medical devices from a power outage is before it starts. Refill prescriptions early, and give anything that needs a fridge or an outlet a backup that doesn't, such as a cooler for insulin or a spare oxygen tank.\n After a hurricane or other disaster, it can be hard to find an open pharmacy, so refill as soon as a storm is in the forecast, and aim for at least 7 to 10 days of every prescription. 1 2 Keep a written list of your medicines, their doses and your device settings. 1 \n Insulin can stay out of the fridge for a limited time if it's kept between 59°F and 86°F: from about 10 days to 8 weeks depending on the product. 3 Check the package insert now, and ask your pharmacist how any other medicine you keep in the fridge holds up without it. 2 3 Heat makes insulin less effective, so if you've had to use insulin that got warmer than 86°F, check your blood sugar more often and throw away that insulin once the emergency is over. 4 Keep it in a cooler with ice packs, but don't let it freeze, since frozen insulin can break down and shouldn't be used. 4 \n A home oxygen concentrator runs on electricity, so ask your supplier for an emergency oxygen tank that needs none. 5 While you're using oxygen, light the house with flashlights rather than candles, to lower the risk of fire. 5 For a concentrator or a CPAP machine, find out from the maker whether it can run on a battery or a generator. 6 Never run a generator inside, even with the windows open, because its exhaust carries carbon monoxide, a poison you can't see or smell that can kill within minutes. 7 8 Set the generator outdoors, more than 20 feet from windows, doors and vents. 7 If your backup is about to run out, or your breathing gets worse, get to a health care facility. 6 \n Dialysis takes the most planning, since no battery or cooler can stand in for a treatment. Ask your center now about its disaster plan and which nearby center could treat you if it closes. 9 When you can't get a treatment, water and waste build up in your body, and rising potassium can affect your heartbeat. 9 10 A strict 3-day emergency diet limits that buildup, so go over it with your center's dietitian and keep the foods on hand. 9 The diet does not replace dialysis, so try to get treatment within 3 days of your last one. 9 \n Sources\n [1] Virginia Department of Health. Prescriptions: Prepare Your Medicine Cabinet for an Emergency (Virginia Department of Health blog, 2022). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://www.vdh.virginia.gov/blog/2022/09/22/prescriptions-prepare-your-medicine-cabinet-for-an-emergency/ \n [2] Safe Drug Use After a Natural Disaster (FDA). August 28, 2019. Accessed August 21, 2026. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/emergency-preparedness-drugs/safe-drug-use-after-natural-disaster \n [3] Wisconsin Department of Health Services. Insulin Storage (Wisconsin Department of Health Services, P-01904). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://dhs.wisconsin.gov/publications/p01904.pdf \n [4] Managing Insulin in an Emergency (CDC). May 15, 2024. Accessed August 21, 2026. https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/articles/managing-insulin-in-emergency.html \n [5] American Thoracic Society. Oxygen Therapy (ATS Patient Education). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://www.thoracic.org/patients/patient-resources/resources/oxygen-therapy.pdf \n [6] American Thoracic Society. Emergency or Disaster: Be Prepared (ATS Patient Education). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://www.thoracic.org/patients/patient-resources/resources/emergency-or-disaster.pdf \n [7] CDC. Carbon Monoxide Poisoning Basics. 2024. Accessed August 8, 2026. https://www.cdc.gov/carbon-monoxide/about/index.html \n [8] Generators and Engine-Driven Tools (CPSC Carbon Monoxide Safety Guide). Accessed August 21, 2026. https://www.cpsc.gov/Safety-Education/Safety-Guides/Carbon-Monoxide-Home/Generators-and-Engine-Driven-Tools \n [9] Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Preparing for Emergencies: A Guide for People on Dialysis (CMS, v2.3 June 2026, hosted by IPRO ESRD Network). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://esrd.ipro.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/CMSPreparingforEmergencies_508-062026.pdf \n [10] National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases. Eating & Nutrition for Hemodialysis (NIDDK). Accessed October 8, 2026. https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/kidney-failure/hemodialysis/eating-nutrition \n About the author\n Dr. Colin Little\n MD, FAWM, Chief Medical Officer, GOES Health\n Board Certified Emergency Physician\n Fellow of the Academy of Wilderness Medicine (FAWM)\n Wilderness Medicine Fellowship, University of Utah\n Emergency Medicine Residency and Chief Resident, Stanford / Kaiser\n Doctor of Medicine, University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix\n Topics\n Medications Hurricanes Natural Disasters Respiratory \n Share this answer\n Copy link \n The GOES app\n Guidance when you're out of signal\n Physician-authored protocols for heat illness, smoke, altitude, snakebite and more, available offline.\n Get GOES now \n Keep reading\n Related answers\n Hurricanes Why are the days after a hurricane so dangerous?\n The days after a hurricane are dangerous because of continued flooding, power outages and the cleanup. Each of these brings its own risks: generator fumes, heat without air conditioning, live power lines and the strain heavy work puts on your heart.\n Read Hurricanes Should you evacuate or ride out a hurricane?\n Run from water; hide from wind. Official evacuation orders are based mostly on flooding risk, which is the most dangerous part of a hurricane. So keep a close eye on your local instructions, and leave if they tell you to go. Leave too if you live in a mobile home, even without an order, since they might not be able to handle the wind. And if someone in your home relies on power for medical equipment, to keep medications cool, or needs dialysis then evacuate early.\n Read Hurricanes Does the category of a hurricane tell you how dangerous it is?\n A hurricane's category tells you how strong its winds are, but it's a poor guide to how much water the storm will bring. Most people killed directly by hurricanes die in flooding from rain or in storm surge, the water a storm pushes ashore. And even a Category 1 or 2 storm can bring both, so check your evacuation zone and the rain forecast, not just the category.\n Read",
   "source": "ok"
  },
